AB  - Surveillance involves the organized monitoring of actors to produce information for the purpose of governing. Ubiquitous surveillance has increasingly become the norm, from the closed-circuit television (CCTV) cameras that have become a mundane feature of built landscapes, to geolocation features in social media apps, and the relentless harvesting of personal data by governments and corporations. Over the last decade, the collection of evermore granular data has been accompanied by sophisticated algorithmic analyses that draw associations between individuals, actions, and objects and provide a decision-making matrix about those relationships. Surveillance has become a normal and invisible part of the fabric of everyday life. In this article, the surprise is not that we are being surveilled, but the possibility that we are not. Here, the line between watcher and watched is relational, shifting from moment to moment. Despite the polycentric, distributed character of much of the new surveillance, geographers have been central to exploring the profound unevenness of the use of surveillance, its effects, and experiences. © 2020 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.
